====================
Livepatch Self Tests
====================

This is a small set of sanity tests for the kernel livepatching.

The test suite loads and unloads several test kernel modules to verify
livepatch behavior.  Debug information is logged to the kernel's message
buffer and parsed for expected messages.  (Note: the tests will compare
the message buffer for only the duration of each individual test.)


Config
------

Set CONFIG_LIVEPATCH=y option and it's prerequisites.


Building the tests
------------------

To only build the tests without running them, run:

  % make -C tools/testing/selftests/livepatch

The command above will compile all test modules and test programs, making them
ready to be packaged if so desired.

Running the tests
-----------------

Test kernel modules are built before running the livepatch selftests.  The
modules are located under test_modules directory, and are built as out-of-tree
modules.  This is specially useful since the same sources can be built and
tested on systems with different kABI, ensuring they the tests are backwards
compatible.  The modules will be loaded by the test scripts using insmod.

To run the livepatch selftests, from the top of the kernel source tree:

  % make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=livepatch run_tests

or

  % make kselftest TARGETS=livepatch


Adding tests
------------

See the common functions.sh file for the existing collection of utility
functions, most importantly setup_config(), start_test() and
check_result().  The latter function greps the kernel's ring buffer for
"livepatch:" and "test_klp" strings, so tests be sure to include one of
those strings for result comparison.  Other utility functions include
general module loading and livepatch loading helpers (waiting for patch
transitions, sysfs entries, etc.)

HDN delayed relocation fixture (not part of automatic TEST_PROGS)
--------------------------------------------------------------
Build test_modules against a CONFIG_SECURITY_HARDENING_PGTABLE=y,
CONFIG_LIVEPATCH=y kernel. Preserve the unsigned test_hdn_klp_patch.ko, then run:

  python3 make-hdn-relocs.py test_modules/test_hdn_klp_patch.ko /tmp/hdn-klp-good.ko

Sign that output and test_hdn_klp_target.ko with the disposable guest's trusted
module key, using that kernel's scripts/sign-file. Do not alter a signed module.
Inside the disposable guest run:

  bash test-hdn-relocs.sh /path/hdn-klp-good.ko /path/test_hdn_klp_target.ko

The fixture checks values through actual module callbacks, not just successful
insertion: one readonly pointer and one eight-byte pointer crossing a page at
an unaligned offset must resolve to the same unexported target. An initial
post-binding callback failure must leave the target unloaded and the journal
clear; two subsequent target loads must bind successfully. The three callback
observations are counted through a read-only module parameter. No force-unload
or livepatch force-transition operation is used.

The converter also writes separate --mode bounds, overlap, metadata and missing
variants. Rewrite the original unsigned file separately for every case, then
sign each output. Bounds/overlap/metadata variants must reject patch insertion
without leaving the patch module loaded. Metadata mode requires RAP metadata in
the input. Missing must load the patch while the target is absent, reject target
insertion with zero binding_count, and permit disabling/removing the patch.
After each rejection, load the valid pair to establish that failure did not
poison later admission. Run the existing livepatch/callback/syscall/replace
suites as regression coverage, including a profile with PGTABLE disabled.
These fixtures and commands have been written but have not been executed.
